{
  "case_id": "2009-08-674",
  "document_hash": "14dd9afffb77934de9800e5e674cb4d0853b77a02a4c214e90ba37725336c66c",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2009-08-674",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "14dd9afffb77934de9800e5e674cb4d0853b77a02a4c214e90ba37725336c66c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-14dd9afffb77-0000-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: We'll hear argument first today in Case 08-674, NRG Power Marketing v. Maine Public Utilities Commission. Mr. Lamken."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioners (Jeffrey A. Lamken)",
      "case_id": "2009-08-674",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "14dd9afffb77934de9800e5e674cb4d0853b77a02a4c214e90ba37725336c66c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-14dd9afffb77-0000-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. LAMKEN: Thank you, Mr. Chief Justice, and may it please the Court: This case concerns a bedrock principle of Federal -- Federal energy law, the Mobile-Sierra doctrine. The question presented and the issue decided below is whether Mobile-Sierra's public interest standard ceases to apply whenever a contract rate is challenged by a noncontracting party. FERC and we agree that the answer is no. Mobile-Sierra's presumption of --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioners (Jeffrey A. Lamken)",
      "case_id": "2009-08-674",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "14dd9afffb77934de9800e5e674cb4d0853b77a02a4c214e90ba37725336c66c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-14dd9afffb77-0000-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 6,
        "start": 6
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Circuit Court's reasoning was very simple: You're a contracting party; you're bound to it. Why should the others who didn't agree to this term be bound by Mobile-Sierra? That's the essence of their holding."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kennedy",
      "case_id": "2009-08-674",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "14dd9afffb77934de9800e5e674cb4d0853b77a02a4c214e90ba37725336c66c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-14dd9afffb77-0000-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 9,
        "start": 9
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KENNEDY: Am I correct -- and maybe this is more proper for the government than for you, or for all the parties. I take it no one questions the propriety, the lawfulness, of the FERC determination to convene the settlement process? There's no argument that this was an improper, an unlawful process?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2009-08-674",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "14dd9afffb77934de9800e5e674cb4d0853b77a02a4c214e90ba37725336c66c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-14dd9afffb77-0000-0006",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 14,
        "start": 14
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOTOMAYOR: It assumed it, so you're asking us to assume the same thing and announce --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Respondent FERC (Eric D. Miller)",
      "case_id": "2009-08-674",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "14dd9afffb77934de9800e5e674cb4d0853b77a02a4c214e90ba37725336c66c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-14dd9afffb77-0000-0009",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Second, the purpose of the public interest test under Mobile-Sierra is to allow contract modification only when it's necessary to protect third parties; that is, members of the public. So it doesn't make sense to say that that test is inapplicable whenever you have a challenge that's presented by one of those third parties or a member of the public."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2009-08-674",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "14dd9afffb77934de9800e5e674cb4d0853b77a02a4c214e90ba37725336c66c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-14dd9afffb77-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 22,
        "start": 22
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Could they come in and show that between the contracting parties the price is unfair? Is that what this is about, that they would try to come in and somehow define the public interest as being informed by the unfairness to the individual parties?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2009-08-674",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "14dd9afffb77934de9800e5e674cb4d0853b77a02a4c214e90ba37725336c66c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-14dd9afffb77-0001-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 22,
        "start": 22
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "What the Court said in Sierra is that mere unfairness, one of the -- the fact that one of the contracting parties got a bad bargain is not a reason to set aside the contract, except for the Court did reserve the extraordinary circumstance where it's going to put the supplier out of business, which would adversely affect the public interest if they can no longer deliver power at that rate."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Respondent",
      "case_id": "2009-08-674",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "14dd9afffb77934de9800e5e674cb4d0853b77a02a4c214e90ba37725336c66c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-14dd9afffb77-0001-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 30,
        "start": 30
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "There is one central truth here on which we and the government agree. These are not contract rates at issue here. They are tariff rates."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2009-08-674",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "14dd9afffb77934de9800e5e674cb4d0853b77a02a4c214e90ba37725336c66c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-14dd9afffb77-0001-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Do you support the opinion of the -- of the court below?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Government",
      "case_id": "2009-08-674",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "14dd9afffb77934de9800e5e674cb4d0853b77a02a4c214e90ba37725336c66c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-14dd9afffb77-0002-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 41,
        "start": 41
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "And the government's position on certiorari was that there should be a remand without reversal for reconsideration in light of Morgan Stanley."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2009-08-674",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "14dd9afffb77934de9800e5e674cb4d0853b77a02a4c214e90ba37725336c66c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-14dd9afffb77-0002-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 44,
        "start": 44
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Has anybody before --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2009-08-674",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "14dd9afffb77934de9800e5e674cb4d0853b77a02a4c214e90ba37725336c66c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-14dd9afffb77-0002-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 46,
        "start": 46
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Under what circumstances could a third party challenge that rate as not fair and reasonable, as opposed to being contrary to the public interest?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2009-08-674",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "14dd9afffb77934de9800e5e674cb4d0853b77a02a4c214e90ba37725336c66c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-14dd9afffb77-0002-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 54,
        "start": 54
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "they were parties to the settlement process simply shows that they've met the sort of broad administrative law standing requirements. It does not show they were an actual purchaser under the agreements here."
    },
    {
